Q: Using execCommand (Javascript) to copy hidden text to clipboard A: <pre><code>const textoPrincipal = document.getElementById('textoPrincipal');

// Clears the text
limparBtn.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {
    textoPrincipal.value = '';
});

// Copies text to the clipboard
copiarBtn.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {
    textoPrincipal.select();
    document.execCommand('copy');
    alert('✅ Texto copiado!');
});
</code></pre>

Q: How to deal with bad_alloc in C++? A: <p>It is often reasonable and useful to catch <code>bad_alloc</code>.  For example, a server may need to allocate and free many blocks of code in servicing a client request.  Some requests may require more memory than others.  It is tedious and error prone to try to check each allocation as it happens.  It is easier and more reliable to simply <code>try</code> to service the request, and if that raises <code>bad_alloc</code>, inform the client of insufficient memory.  The server then continues on, as usual.</p>
<pre><code>try {
  service_request();
}
catch (const std::bad_alloc&amp;) {
  // inform client of insufficient memory
  ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>It seems to me that this is exactly the kind of thing C++ exceptions are meant for.</p>
<p>Separately, while some systems can overcommit memory allocation requests, many do not.  Those that allow it can be configured to not do so (which I highly recommend).  Note that delayed virtual page instantiation is <em>different than</em> overcommitting memory.  Delayed instantiation is common; the system can account for the virtual memory page, while still waiting until it is used to incur the overhead of instantiating it.  This is not a fault of any kind; it is normal operation.</p>

Q: Implementing GetHashCode correctly A: <p>Let's say your class looks like this:</p>

<pre><code>class Frob {
    public string Foo { get; set; }
    public int Bar { get; set; }
    public double FooBar { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Let's say you define equals so that two instances of <code>Frob</code> are equal if their <code>Foo</code> and their <code>Bar</code> are equal, but <code>FooBar</code> doesn't matter.</p>

<p>Then you should define <code>GetHashCode</code> in terms of <code>Foo</code> and <code>Bar</code>. One way is like this:</p>

<pre><code>return this.Foo.GetHashCode() * 17 + this.Bar.GetHashCode();
</code></pre>

<p>Basically, you just want to incorporate all the fields that go into defining the equality. One way is to just keep accumulating and multiplying by 17 like I've done. It's fast, it's simple, it's correct, and it usually gives a good distribution.</p>
